of build-time caching (correct, per the comment — source config is genuinely request-time), the safety net should be React's per-request `cache()`, not a longer-lived cache:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => { ... });\n```\n\nThis costs nothing today and prevents an easy regression later (e.g., both a layout and a page independently needing the source list).\n\n**3. `getDb()` connection caching is already done correctly** — worth calling out as a positive, not a bug: `lib/db/index.ts:22-44` caches the postgres-js client on `globalThis` specifically to survive HMR reloads in dev. That's the right pattern; don't touch it.\n\n## Nothing to report (checked and clean)\n- **N+1 queries**: only one query path exists (`listSources`), no per-row follow-up queries anywhere.\n- **React re-renders**: `NavRail`, `SourceTable`, `StepList`, `PipelineStrip` — all render small, static-shaped arrays (≤6 items). `useMemo`/`React.memo` here would be pure ceremony with zero measurable benefit.\n- **Memory leaks**: no `useEffect`, `setInterval`, `setTimeout`, or `addEventListener` anywhere in the codebase (verified via grep) — nothing to clean up because nothing subscribes to anything yet.\n- **Redundant computation**: `source.config.objects.join(\", \")` in the table render is trivial and re-runs at most a few times a session; not worth memoizing.\n\n## What to design for before you build `/runs` and `/conflicts`\n\nThe schema (`lib/db/schema.ts`) is exactly the shape where N+1 bugs show up first: `matches.entityAId`/`entityBId` → `entities`, and `conflicts.matchId` → `matches`. When you build the conflict queue, the naive version will be:\n\n```ts\n// AVOID: N+1 — one query per conflict to resolve its match, then two more for its entities\nconst conflicts = await db.select().from(conflictsTable);\nfor (const c of conflicts) {\n  const match = await db.select().from(matches).where(eq(matches.id, c.matchId));\n  const entityA = await db.select().from(entities).where(eq(entities.id, match.entityAId));\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nInstead, join once with Drizzle's relational query API or explicit joins:\n\n```ts\nconst rows = await db\n  .select()\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .innerJoin(matches, eq(conflicts.matchId, matches.id))\n  .innerJoin(entities, eq(matches.entityAId, entities.id))\n  .where(eq(conflicts.runId, runId));\n```\n\nI'd flag this in review when that page lands — it's the one place in this domain where N+1 is a near-certainty if written the obvious way.\n\n**Bottom line**: nothing urgent to fix right now beyond the two small `sources.ts` tweaks above. The real performance risk in this app is in code that doesn't exist yet (the conflict/entity list views) — that's the place to apply query-join discipline up front rather than retrofit it.\n",
